  "id": "DrugBank_DB11160 - MONDO_grouped_2309_2308",
  "verbalization": "Phenyltoloxamine, a drug with a known indication for urticaria and angioedema, is linked to Papillary Conjunctivitis through a chain of indications involving Promethazine and Alcaftadine. The evidence suggests that Phenyltoloxamine may exert a therapeutic effect on Papillary Conjunctivitis via a mechanism that involves the suppression of allergic reactions, as indicated by its association with urticaria and angioedema. The shared pathway through Promethazine and Alcaftadine implies a potential role in modulating inflammatory responses, possibly by inhibiting histamine release or other mediators of allergic inflammation. The final scores from the three paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism remains partially inferred. The presence of overlapping indications and the involvement of Promethazine, a known antihistamine, supports the hypothesis that Phenyltoloxamine may act through a similar mechanism in the context of Papillary Conjunctivitis.",
